AI powered threat detection:
  AI powered threat detection uses machine learning algorithms to monitor network traffic, user behaviour and system logs to identify patterns that indicate an attack
  is occuring.
  Firstly, the system learns what baseline, regular traffic looks like for each user, device and application in the network. Any deviation from the baseline by the system
  triggers an alert. AI agents collect any alerts, recommending actions to be taken, or in extreme cases, they can take action themselves. For example, Agentic SOCs.
  Agentic SOCs (Security Operations Centres) are AI powered SOCs that are capable of autonomous reasoning and response with reduced need for human interaction. They respond
  to anomalous activities based on a pre-defined and approved response plan, executing incident responses quicker than humanly possible, reducing the damage of an attack.
  Agentic SOCs do still require human interaction to interpret the AI's insites, and approve or ignore these suggestions.
  Source:
    Stellar Cyber. (2026, February 23). What is agentic SOC? Complete guide. https://stellarcyber.ai/learn/what-is-agentic-soc/
Post-quantum cryptography:
  With the rise in quantum computing, algorithms currently used to encrypt data won't be strong enough to ensure the security of private data. Quantum computing can easily
  crack encryption algorithms that are too computationally expensive to be run by a non-quantum computer. As such, to protect banking information and other private data in the
  future, stronger algorithms must be designed that are too computationally expensive to crack, even for quantum computers. At the moment, large amounts of data are being 
  collected by adversaries to be decrypted when quantum computers become widely available, at which time the data will be used as leverage. The current solution to this is to
  implement encryption that relies on lattice based mathematics. Lattice based mathematics uses matrices and matrix opertations for things like the PQC equivilant of Diffie-Hellman.
  Instead of using integers to calculate the keys, Lattice based approaches, like ML-KEM (Kyber), use matrices instead of integers. While this requires more bits during a key
  exchange, it is post-quantum secure.
  Sources: 
    Patton, C., & Schwabe, P. (2025, March 25). Prepping for post-quantum: A beginner’s guide to lattice cryptography. The Cloudflare Blog. https://blog.cloudflare.com/lattice-crypto-primer/
